  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nNortheasterly winds become breezy and temperatures plummet areawide \nbehind Thursday night/Friday morning's front. Strong cold air \nadvection keeps highs in the upper 50s and low 60s across west Texas \nand southeast New Mexico Friday afternoon. As mentioned in the Short \nTerm, high gap winds will be possible in the Guadalupe and Delaware \nMountains following the front through Saturday morning. The front \nalso pushes the best rain chances (50-80%) southward towards the \nDavis Mountains, Big Bend, Presidio Valley, and Lower Trans-Pecos \nFriday morning and into the afternoon. By late Friday afternoon and \nearly evening, winds generally begin to weaken and rain chances \ndiminish across the area. Saturday morning will feature some of the \ncoldest temperatures we have seen so far this season. Lows dip into \nthe 40s for most locations, with upper 30s in the higher terrain and \nusual cold spots. Continued northeasterly winds keep Saturday's \nhighs similar to Friday's, albeit a degree or two warmer. Likewise, \nlows Sunday morning end up a degree or two warmer overall than \nSaturday morning.\n\nFrom Sunday through at least the middle of next week, a more robust \nwarming trend develops. Highs Sunday top out in the 60s and low 70s \nfor most as winds veer to the south again. By Monday and Tuesday, \ntemperatures in the mid-to-upper 70s become commonplace. As far as \nrain goes, from Saturday through early next week most locations look \nto stay dry, with chances staying generally below 15%. By the middle \nof next week, the NBM reintroduces low (10-30%) rain chances for \nportions of the area as another system nears. However, differences \nin the timing and location of this system exist between long range \nmodels, which keeps confidence low at this time.\n\nSprang",
